===============================================================================
SCOPE — READ THIS BEFORE REUSING ANY FILE
===============================================================================

The MIT grant above covers ONLY this project's own original files. It does
NOT apply repository-wide, and for most of the `usbd_*` files it does not
apply at all.

Covered by MIT (this project's own work):

  main.cpp, usbd_irq.c, usbd_compat.h, the application layer of
  usbd_audio_if.c/h, the descriptor bytes and composite-device structure in
  usbd_audio.c/h, the tests under tests/, read_transport_diagnostics.py,
  read_sai_clock.py, read_chip_rev.py, gen_table.py, the scripts under
  scripts/, and the project documentation.

  usbd_compat.h is included by ST-derived files but contains no ST code: it
  defines accessor macros that dispatch on the declared type of two
  USBD_HandleTypeDef fields, plus assertions rejecting an unrecognised
  layout. Naming a structure's fields to read them is use of an interface,
  not reproduction of the code declaring it.

NOT covered — third-party code under its own terms:

  * STMicroelectronics, SLA0044 (LICENSE-SLA0044.txt).
    usbd_ctlreq.c is ST v2.5.2 with two documented deviations -- the
    hardcoded 4U interface bound, and the pClass accesses routed through
    usbd_compat.h so the file builds against both generations of ST's
    middleware. Both are described in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md; nothing else
    in that file differs from upstream. The class-callback contract and the
    descriptor structures and macros in usbd_audio.h, and the fops-table
    shape and IntToUnicode/Get_SerialNum helpers in usbd_desc.c, are ST's.
    SLA0044 restricts execution to ST silicon, and its section 5 forbids
    subjecting THAT software to Open Source Terms — a category it defines
    broadly enough to name BSD explicitly. MIT is functionally equivalent to
    BSD. This is precisely why the grant above is scoped to this project's
    own files and is never extended to ST's: applying MIT to ST's files
    would breach section 5. Do not "simplify" this by declaring the whole
    repository MIT.

  * Electro-Smith libDaisy, MIT (LICENSE-libDaisy-MIT.txt).
    usbd_desc.h and the base of usbd_conf.c/h arrive through libDaisy, which
    redistributes ST files under its own MIT while those files retain their
    ST headers internally — so they carry both obligations. libDaisy's
    notice must be reproduced in any distribution.

  * ST HAL and CMSIS, BSD-3-Clause. Not present in this tree; reached
    through libDaisy.

THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md is the authoritative file-by-file breakdown and was
verified by direct comparison against stm32_mw_usb_device v2.5.2, libDaisy
master, and STM32CubeF4 AUDIO_Standalone — not inferred from file headers.
Check it for the specific file before vendoring or redistributing anything
from here.
